1. S3 Folder Structure
A good artifact bucket could look like:

my-company-artifacts/
│
├── dev/
│   ├── app/
│   └── config/
│
├── staging/
│   ├── app/
│   └── config/
│
└── prod/
    ├── app/
    │   ├── v1.0.0/
    │   ├── v1.1.0/
    │   └── v1.2.0/
    │
    └── config/



2. S3 Security Checklist
For a production bucket, think about:

S3 Security
│
├── Block Public Access
├── IAM least privilege
├── Bucket policies
├── Encryption
├── Versioning
├── Logging / auditing
├── Lifecycle policies
├── Object ownership
├── Access points where useful
└── Monitoring


3. CORS = Cross-Origin Resource Sharing.

In S3, CORS is mainly relevant when a browser-based application hosted on one origin wants to access objects in an S3 bucket from JavaScript.

Real-world example

Suppose your frontend is:

https://www.myapp.com

Your images/files are stored in:

https://my-app-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com

The browser sees:

myapp.com
     ↓
     ↓ JavaScript request
     ↓
S3 bucket

These are different origins.

The browser's same-origin security rules therefore come into play.

1. What is an Origin?

An origin consists of:

Protocol + Domain + Port

For example:

https://www.myapp.com:443

is an origin.

These are different origins:

https://www.myapp.com
https://api.myapp.com
https://mybucket.s3.amazonaws.com

Even though they may belong to the same company.

2. Real-time S3 CORS example ⭐

Imagine you build a React application:

React Frontend
https://shop.example.com

Users upload profile pictures directly to S3:

Browser
   │
   │ PUT image
   ▼
S3

Your JavaScript might make a request like:

fetch("https://mybucket.s3.amazonaws.com/profile.jpg", {
    method: "PUT"
});

The browser asks:

"Is shop.example.com allowed to make this request to S3?"

S3's CORS configuration tells the browser which cross-origin requests are allowed.

3. Example CORS configuration

Conceptually:

{
  "CORSRules": [
    {
      "AllowedOrigins": [
        "https://shop.example.com"
      ],
      "AllowedMethods": [
        "GET",
        "PUT"
      ],
      "AllowedHeaders": [
        "*"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

This says:

Origin:
https://shop.example.com

Allowed:
GET
PUT

Headers:
Allowed

So:

shop.example.com
       │
       │ GET / PUT
       ▼
      S3
       │
       ▼
     Allowed ✅
4. What happens without CORS?

Suppose your S3 bucket contains:

photo.jpg

Your React application tries:

https://shop.example.com
          │
          │ JavaScript
          ▼
S3 bucket

If S3 doesn't return the appropriate CORS response headers, the browser blocks the cross-origin response.

You may see an error similar to:

Access to fetch at ...
has been blocked by CORS policy
Important:

The request may actually reach S3.

The browser is enforcing the CORS restriction on whether JavaScript can access the response.
